# Purpose
Explain the shared metal/glass visual language and its wide, phone, compact Fold
and adaptive focus layouts. Teach public authors how to describe useful work,
preserve viewer control and distinguish measured progress from illustrative data.

# Dependencies
- ../reference/agent-iq-metal-starter.html provides a self-contained local example.
- ../CATALOGUE.md and ../README.md route users and agents to this guide.
- ../SANITIZATION.md supplies the public publication boundary.

# Gotchas
- Public authoring guidance only: no private endpoints, identifiers, paths,
  credentials, deployment evidence or incident history.
- Examples are illustrative, never live observations or production metrics.
- Native inert HTML and optional isolated script regions have different
  capabilities. Do not imply local example functions are product APIs.
- Copyable script examples include their own document sizing reset so the
  canvas fills the isolated region without depending on the preview wrapper.
- Read-only design/current-view inspection is conditional on the current tool
  manifest or trusted native API. Isolated examples receive supplied snapshots
  and must never acquire a parent-DOM or network bridge.
- Optional native query keys are omitted when absent, rather than passing an
  undefined own property to strict request normalization.
- Startup and example layouts are quality references, not a required workflow
  or fixed number of panels. Respect actual available width and height.

# Change History
- 2026-09-21: Added "Spaces beside the frame" — the four dockable spaces
  (Topology, Evidence, Before·After, Architecture), the reader controls (chip
  toggle, three-pane tiling, long-press drag to any edge, keyboard moves,
  reset, per-device persistence), the advisory scene-root layout attributes
  (data-gv-iq-spaces / data-gv-iq-layout / data-gv-iq-share; display-only,
  honoured while following, human veto until the next turn) and the
  Architecture-space declaration primitives (data-gv-iq-arch v1 rows with
  node/kind/heat/deps limits; heat is declared focus, never telemetry).
- 2026-09-20: Created a public design guide with reusable code and layout advice.
- 2026-09-21: Added the "Advanced graphics with motion" subsection — animated SVG
  + SMIL is the standard rich-graphics vehicle (reveal-on-land, turning ring,
  pulsing status dot, self-contained flowing connector) — plus a caveat that
  isolated script regions are advanced/optional and may be acknowledged yet not
  visible (prefer SVG). Reframed "Copyable starting code" so the native/animated
  SVG path leads and the script-region starter is the optional/advanced example.
